---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const a = appBySlug('mailchimp')!;
const b = appBySlug('sendlane')!;
const path = '/compare/mailchimp-vs-sendlane';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-sendlane');
const desc = description(site, 'compare/mailchimp-vs-sendlane');
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Must Mailchimp cart flows be recreated?",
    "answer": "Mailchimp\u2019s Shopify connection guide says existing abandoned-cart automations need to be recreated. Preserve the account records, but verify the new flow separately."
  },
  {
    "question": "Are cart and checkout abandonment the same trigger?",
    "answer": "No. Sendlane documents separate cart and checkout automations. Decide which takes precedence when a shopper advances to checkout."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can Mailchimp send cart SMS?",
    "answer": "Yes, subject to supported integration and approved SMS program requirements. Its guide limits recovery to one SMS per cart within 48 hours of flow entry."
  },
  {
    "question": "What makes migration ready?",
    "answer": "Reconcile subscription status, event entry, purchase exits and sender ownership using identifiable test records before enabling the replacement."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
<ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
<section class="py-14 md:py-20"><div class="container">
<a href="/compare" class="link">← Comparisons</a>
<h1 class="hero-title mt-6">{a.name} vs {b.name} for Shopify cart recovery</h1>
<p class="mt-7 text-xl">Keep an established audience or rebuild recovery around Shopify events: account continuity is the decision.</p>
<TldrBox picks={[{"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Mailchimp", "reason": "Retain an established audience and validate its store-connected flows."}, {"label": "Evaluate", "tool": "Sendlane", "reason": "Rebuild cart and checkout automation with explicit event precedence."}]} />
<article class="prose-shopify mt-12 max-w-4xl">

<h2>Start with the account you would have to unwind</h2>
<p>Choose Mailchimp when retaining your established audience, templates and campaign operations matters more than changing the automation model. Evaluate Sendlane when the team is prepared to rebuild recovery around separate Shopify cart and checkout events. Neither choice earns a recovery winner without store-level evidence. An account migration is a change to subscription records and live flows, not just a template export.</p>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/connect-shopify/">Mailchimp’s Shopify connection guide</a> says to review and pause existing welcome automations before connecting, and to recreate existing abandoned-cart automations. Keeping the same account does not guarantee that an old automation remains suitable after reconnecting the store. Record audience, store identifier, live triggers and current senders before changing the connection.</p>
<h2>Compare the actual recovery controls</h2>
<p><a href="https://mailchimp.com/help/create-abandoned-cart-customer-journey/">Mailchimp supports abandoned-cart email and SMS flows</a>. A supported store connection is required; SMS needs an approved program and specific setup. The guide permits one recovery SMS per cart within 48 hours of entry. Additional email or SMS steps have subscribed-contact restrictions. Validate the intended audience and channel eligibility rather than inheriting a generic three-touch ladder.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.sendlane.com/articles/2061739721-how-to-set-up-an-abandoned-checkout-automation">Sendlane’s checkout setup</a> recommends at least one hour before considering checkout abandoned; its five- and thirty-minute settings are for internal testing. It documents ending browse and cart automations when checkout takes over, plus an order-based exit spanning the flow duration. This is useful when your problem is overlapping intent stages, but those nodes still need configuring.</p>
<h2>Build a continuity ledger before switching</h2>
<p>Export the records you need to explain who can receive what: contact identifier, email and phone, channel subscription status, source, suppression state and relevant timestamps. Map the source audience or list to the intended destination. Keep unsubscribes suppressed; do not convert a past order or an imported phone number into a new marketing subscription. Retain a dated export and a field mapping so a later discrepancy can be traced.</p>
<p><a href="https://help.sendlane.com/articles/4961381973-how-to-integrate-shopify-and-sendlane">Sendlane’s integration guide</a> describes connecting Shopify behavior and purchase history for segmentation and automations. Before treating historical data as ready, inspect a recent repeat buyer, a refunded order and a newly unsubscribed customer in the destination. A successful app installation alone does not demonstrate that your particular records or flow exclusions survived.</p>
<h2>Prove cart-to-checkout precedence with one customer</h2>
<p>Use an identifiable test customer on mobile. Add a bundle to cart, then proceed to checkout and abandon it. Record which event arrived, which flow entered and whether the earlier cart reminder stopped. Follow the recovery link and check variant, quantity, bundle price and any gift message. Complete a purchase while a later reminder is waiting and confirm that it exits. Test a new cart separately to see whether your chosen re-entry setting behaves as intended.</p>
<p>A logged-in repeat buyer should not receive a first-purchase promise. A B2B cart below MOQ needs an explanation or sales handoff; a larger coupon will not make it eligible. For international shipping shock, link to destination-specific shipping information. For BNPL or financing hesitation, offer payment support without promising approval. A subscription toggle needs clear recurring terms. Assign these branches only where your actual event fields can support them; otherwise use accurate general assistance.</p>
<h2>Keep the offer tied to the obstacle</h2>
<p>Illustrative fixed-goods-cost model: a $169 cart at 47% gross margin has $79.43 gross profit before discount. A 20% code costs $33.80, leaving $45.63. A $308 cart at 38% has $117.04; a 17% code costs $52.36, leaving $64.68. These exclude shipping subsidies, payment fees, messaging costs and returns. They are budget examples, not measured recoveries or recommended incentive caps.</p>
<p>Check welcome-code stacking and bundle eligibility before enabling an offer. Route an unavailable variant to a suitable alternative or waitlist rather than repeatedly linking a dead cart. After a partial refund, keep the original recovery flow closed and decide separately whether service follow-up is appropriate. Check sizing and gift questions before applying a discount that cannot resolve them.</p>
<h2>Cut over by flow, then reconcile orders</h2>
<p>Name one active sender for welcome, browse, cart and checkout on each channel, including Shopify’s own automation. Pause the old equivalent before enabling its replacement; save its configuration for rollback. Define who monitors failures and which discrepancy causes a pause. If rollback is needed, inspect waiting contacts before reactivating an old flow so they do not get both sequences.</p>
<p>Compare the same order IDs, net sales, discounts and refunds across the before/after periods. Do not add overlapping platform attribution totals. A suitably designed holdout can help assess incrementality if order volume supports it; no universal holdout percentage or attribution inflation rate follows from these product docs. Obtain a current quote using the same contact count, send volume, SMS usage and support scope, including migration work, before making cost the deciding factor.</p>

<p>Primary documentation checked November 25, 2026. Exercises above are proposed checks, not observed vendor results.</p>
</article>
<FaqSection faqs={faqs} title={`${a.name} vs ${b.name} FAQ`} />
</div></section>
</BaseLayout>
